{"step_index":174,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-16T08:56:10Z","input_tokens":3290,"cache_read_tokens":242598,"output_tokens":8446,"thinking":"Initial check reveals HTML files present in the specified directory. The objective is to generate playable HTML versions of both \"Cách 1\" and \"Cách 2,\" ensuring no existing files are overwritten.\n\n\nTwo new HTML files will be created: `card_sort_cach_1_6cols.html` and `card_sort_cach_2_canvas_5cols.html`. Both versions will incorporate dynamic target formulas, ensuring responsive and playable card sorting with distinct features for each approach. The common mechanics focus on maintaining gameplay balance and clarity within the card sorting.\n\n\nThe target formula dictates the minimum number of consecutive cards of a color required to clear a column, capped at eight. Observing that columns in \"Cách 1\" begin with mixed colors, the initial assumption that an entire column needs to be pure is incorrect. The game design must allow clearing a partial column based on the target, not require a fully pure column for immediate clearing in the first rounds.\n\n\nThe target formula defines the minimum clear threshold for a color within a column. The clearing mechanism is triggered when consecutive cards of the same color at the bottom of a column meet or exceed this target. This allows partial columns to be cleared, which allows for dynamic gameplay. The example demonstrates how merging card stacks can quickly meet the clear threshold, leading to an immediate burst and the clearing of those cards.\n\n\nThe clearing mechanism applies when the color threshold (`k >= Target`) is met, instantly removing consecutive card batches from the bottom up. Empty columns created by this clearing mechanism then function as valid destinations for card moves. Examining Cách 2, the pixel art frame (10x10) is split, with each pair of pixel columns mapped to a corresponding card column below, and each column initially receives the first four cards from the bottom of the image.\n\n\nThe automatic dispensing mechanism for \"Cách 2\" involves pixel art. When a card column is depleted (fewer than four cards or empty), and the corresponding pixel column above still has pixels, the bottom pixel from the image drops down as a new card into the column. This \"harvesting\" effect is visualized as the pixel disappearing from the image in real-time as a card is added. The pixel should drop onto the top (back) of column $i$.\n\n\nThe dispensing mechanism should replenish an empty column by pushing new cards to its top, simulating a gravity-fed system. This replenishing behavior is triggered either when a column becomes empty or falls below a threshold, with the painting above supplying the new cards. This mechanism introduces strategic implications, creating opportunities for empty columns, impacting gameplay balance. The question remains about the replenishment threshold and its effect on gameplay.\n\n\nThe replenishment mechanism will add new cards to a column when the card count falls below a threshold, triggered by `col.splice(...)` or when `col.length` is less than 3, with each drop simulating a gravity-fed card refill from the pixel art painting.  The insertion point for the new card is at the top/back using `col.unshift(pixel)`, ensuring that the new cards do not interfere with the player's current card matching. This ensures smooth, bottom-up pixel-to-card gameplay. The game design should therefore include both mechanisms.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":false,\"Summary\":\"Playable HTML prototype for Method 1 (card_sort_cach_1_6cols.html): 6 wide columns (58px), compressed pixel upper cards, separated bottom Touch Pad card displaying dynamic k/Target counter badge, and 1 single-color multi-card holding slot.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","Content":"\"<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html lang=\\\"vi\\\">\\n<head>\\n  <meta c